# Google Cluster Data 2011 - Schema Reference

## Overview
The Google Cluster Data 2011-2 trace represents 29 days of Borg cell information from May 2011, on a cluster of about 12,500 machines. The trace starts at 19:00 EDT on Sunday May 1, 2011.

## Timestamp Format
All timestamps are in **microseconds** since 600 seconds before the beginning of the trace period, represented as 64-bit integers.

Example: An event 20 seconds after the trace start would have timestamp = 620,000,000 microseconds.

## Job Events Table Schema

CSV Format with the following fields:

| Field Index | Field Name | Type | Description |
|-------------|------------|------|-------------|
| 0 | timestamp | long | Time of event in microseconds |
| 1 | missing info | string | Indicator for missing data |
| 2 | job ID | long | Unique 64-bit job identifier |
| 3 | event type | int | Type of event (see Event Types below) |
| 4 | user | string | Hashed username |
| 5 | scheduling class | int | Latency sensitivity (0=free, 1=free, 2=best-effort, 3=production) |
| 6 | job name | string | Hashed job name (often unique) |
| 7 | logical job name | string | Hashed logical job name |

### Job Event Types
- **0** = SUBMIT - Job became eligible for scheduling
- **1** = SCHEDULE - Job was scheduled
- **2** = EVICT - Job was descheduled due to higher priority job
- **3** = FAIL - Job failed
- **4** = FINISH - Job completed successfully
- **5** = KILL - Job was killed
- **6** = LOST - Job lost due to machine failure
- **7** = UPDATE_PENDING - Job update pending
- **8** = UPDATE_RUNNING - Job update running

### Example Job Event Record
```csv
5629483964,,6252018749,4,68CsCZngOkuQn5lVo+dNXKGoFx9ZmJXWR+W7NAUmEH4=,1,C2lS27hTHgbw7EjMMKKnvw2Rwd9VuZoZBuvZhuM7CC4=,XfCpNUTe13nQeRRun9yEceT55eMsoXrXhE0fUiq+Dfs=
```
- Timestamp: 5629483964
- Missing info: (empty)
- Job ID: 6252018749
- Event type: 4 (FINISH)
- User: 68CsCZngOkuQn5lVo+dNXKGoFx9ZmJXWR+W7NAUmEH4=
- Scheduling class: 1
- Job name: C2lS27hTHgbw7EjMMKKnvw2Rwd9VuZoZBuvZhuM7CC4=
- Logical job name: XfCpNUTe13nQeRRun9yEceT55eMsoXrXhE0fUiq+Dfs=

## Task Events Table Schema

CSV Format with the following fields:

| Field Index | Field Name | Type | Description |
|-------------|------------|------|-------------|
| 0 | timestamp | long | Time of event in microseconds |
| 1 | missing info | string | Indicator for missing data |
| 2 | job ID | long | Unique 64-bit job identifier |
| 3 | task index | int | 0-based index identifying task within job |
| 4 | machine ID | long | Unique machine identifier (if scheduled) |
| 5 | event type | int | Type of event (see Event Types below) |
| 6 | user | string | Hashed username |
| 7 | scheduling class | int | Latency sensitivity |
| 8 | priority | int | Priority (0-11, higher = more important) |
| 9 | CPU request | float | Requested CPU cores |
| 10 | memory request | float | Requested memory (0-1 scale) |
| 11 | disk space request | float | Requested disk space |
| 12 | different machines restriction | boolean | Whether task must run on different machine than others |

### Task Event Types
- **0** = SUBMIT - Task became eligible for scheduling
- **1** = SCHEDULE - Task was scheduled on a machine
- **2** = EVICT - Task was descheduled due to higher priority task
- **3** = FAIL - Task failed
- **4** = FINISH - Task completed successfully
- **5** = KILL - Task was killed
- **6** = LOST - Task lost due to machine failure
- **7** = UPDATE_PENDING - Task update pending
- **8** = UPDATE_RUNNING - Task update running

### Example Task Event Record
```csv
5611824441,,6251812952,1761,1306108,4,fJYeclskJqPWsAT6TX/r9X5OiIZpSEb2PBGliYAOMxM=,0,2,0.02499,0.07959,0.0003862,1
```
- Timestamp: 5611824441 microseconds
- Missing info: (empty)
- Job ID: 6251812952
- Task index: 1761
- Machine ID: 1306108
- Event type: 4 (FINISH)
- User: fJYeclskJqPWsAT6TX/r9X5OiIZpSEb2PBGliYAOMxM=
- Scheduling class: 0
- Priority: 2
- CPU request: 0.02499
- Memory request: 0.07959
- Disk space request: 0.0003862
- Different machines restriction: 1

## Parsing CSV Records

### Java Example - Job Event
```java
public class JobEvent {
    private long timestamp;
    private String missingInfo;
    private long jobId;
    private int eventType;
    private String user;
    private int schedulingClass;
    private String jobName;
    private String logicalJobName;

    public static JobEvent fromCsv(String line) {
        String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);
        JobEvent event = new JobEvent();
        event.timestamp = Long.parseLong(fields[0]);
        event.missingInfo = fields[1];
        event.jobId = Long.parseLong(fields[2]);
        event.eventType = Integer.parseInt(fields[3]);
        event.user = fields[4];
        event.schedulingClass = Integer.parseInt(fields[5]);
        event.jobName = fields[6];
        event.logicalJobName = fields[7];
        return event;
    }
}
```

### Java Example - Task Event
```java
public class TaskEvent {
    private long timestamp;
    private String missingInfo;
    private long jobId;
    private int taskIndex;
    private long machineId;
    private int eventType;
    private String user;
    private int schedulingClass;
    private int priority;
    private float cpuRequest;
    private float memoryRequest;
    private float diskRequest;
    private int differentMachinesRestriction;

    public static TaskEvent fromCsv(String line) {
        String[] fields = line.split(",", -1);
        TaskEvent event = new TaskEvent();
        event.timestamp = Long.parseLong(fields[0]);
        event.missingInfo = fields[1];
        event.jobId = Long.parseLong(fields[2]);
        event.taskIndex = Integer.parseInt(fields[3]);
        event.machineId = fields[4].isEmpty() ? -1 : Long.parseLong(fields[4]);
        event.eventType = Integer.parseInt(fields[5]);
        event.user = fields[6];
        event.schedulingClass = Integer.parseInt(fields[7]);
        event.priority = Integer.parseInt(fields[8]);
        event.cpuRequest = Float.parseFloat(fields[9]);
        event.memoryRequest = Float.parseFloat(fields[10]);
        event.diskRequest = Float.parseFloat(fields[11]);
        event.differentMachinesRestriction = Integer.parseInt(fields[12]);
        return event;
    }
}
```

## Common Query Patterns

### Filter for SUBMIT events
```java
stream.filter(event -> event.getEventType() == 0);
```

### Group by Job ID
```java
stream.keyBy(event -> event.getJobId());
```

### Detect job completion
```java
stream.filter(event -> event.getEventType() == 4); // FINISH
```

## Important Notes

1. **Timestamps are in microseconds** - Convert to seconds by dividing by 1,000,000
2. **Job IDs are unique** - A job keeps its ID throughout its lifetime
3. **Task indices are 0-based** - First task in a job has index 0
4. **Missing fields** - Some fields may be empty (represented by empty strings in CSV)
5. **Multiple events per task** - A task can have multiple events (submit, schedule, fail, resubmit, etc.)
6. **Scheduling classes** - 0 and 1 are "free" priority, 9-11 are "production" priority
7. **Data is compressed** - Files are gzipped (.gz extension)

## Resources
- [Google Cluster Data GitHub Repository](https://github.com/google/cluster-data)
- [ClusterData2011_2 Documentation](https://github.com/google/cluster-data/blob/master/ClusterData2011_2.md)
- [Format + Schema PDF](https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B5g07T_gRDg9Z0lsSTEtTWtpOW8/view)

