# Apache Flink KeyedProcessFunction and Timers

## Overview
KeyedProcessFunction is a low-level stream processing operation that provides fine-grained control over state and time. It's ideal for implementing custom logic like session detection, timeout handling, and complex event processing.

## KeyedProcessFunction Basics

### Structure
```java
public class MyProcessFunction
    extends KeyedProcessFunction<KeyType, InputType, OutputType> {

    @Override
    public void processElement(
        InputType value,
        Context ctx,
        Collector<OutputType> out
    ) throws Exception {
        // Process each element
        // Access state
        // Register timers
        // Emit output
    }

    @Override
    public void onTimer(
        long timestamp,
        OnTimerContext ctx,
        Collector<OutputType> out
    ) throws Exception {
        // Called when timer fires
        // Access state
        // Emit output
    }
}
```

### Context Methods
- `ctx.timestamp()` - Get current element's timestamp
- `ctx.timerService()` - Access timer service
- `ctx.getCurrentKey()` - Get current key
- `ctx.output(outputTag, value)` - Emit to side output

## State in KeyedProcessFunction

### ValueState
Store a single value per key:
```java
public class StatefulProcessFunction
    extends KeyedProcessFunction<Long, Event, Result> {

    private transient ValueState<Integer> countState;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) throws Exception {
        ValueStateDescriptor<Integer> descriptor =
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("count", Integer.class, 0);
        countState = getRuntimeContext().getState(descriptor);
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(
        Event value,
        Context ctx,
        Collector<Result> out
    ) throws Exception {
        Integer currentCount = countState.value();
        currentCount++;
        countState.update(currentCount);

        out.collect(new Result(ctx.getCurrentKey(), currentCount));
    }
}
```

### ListState
Store multiple values per key:
```java
public class ListStateFunction
    extends KeyedProcessFunction<Long, Event, Result> {

    private transient ListState<Event> eventsState;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) throws Exception {
        ListStateDescriptor<Event> descriptor =
            new ListStateDescriptor<>("events", Event.class);
        eventsState = getRuntimeContext().getListState(descriptor);
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(
        Event value,
        Context ctx,
        Collector<Result> out
    ) throws Exception {
        // Add to list
        eventsState.add(value);

        // Iterate over all events
        List<Event> allEvents = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Event e : eventsState.get()) {
            allEvents.add(e);
        }

        out.collect(new Result(allEvents.size()));
    }
}
```

### MapState
Store key-value pairs per key:
```java
private transient MapState<String, Integer> mapState;

@Override
public void open(Configuration parameters) throws Exception {
    MapStateDescriptor<String, Integer> descriptor =
        new MapStateDescriptor<>("map", String.class, Integer.class);
    mapState = getRuntimeContext().getMapState(descriptor);
}

@Override
public void processElement(Event value, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out)
    throws Exception {
    // Put value
    mapState.put(value.getKey(), value.getValue());

    // Get value
    Integer val = mapState.get(value.getKey());

    // Iterate
    for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : mapState.entries()) {
        // Process entry
    }

    // Remove
    mapState.remove(value.getKey());
}
```

## Timers

### Event Time Timers
Triggered when watermark passes the timer timestamp:
```java
@Override
public void processElement(Event value, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out)
    throws Exception {
    // Register event time timer
    long timerTime = value.getTimestamp() + 60000; // 60 seconds later
    ctx.timerService().registerEventTimeTimer(timerTime);
}

@Override
public void onTimer(long timestamp, OnTimerContext ctx, Collector<Result> out)
    throws Exception {
    // Timer fired
    out.collect(new Result("Timer fired at " + timestamp));
}
```

### Processing Time Timers
Triggered at wall-clock time:
```java
@Override
public void processElement(Event value, Context ctx, Collector<Result> out)
    throws Exception {
    // Register processing time timer
    long currentTime = ctx.timerService().currentProcessingTime();
    long timerTime = currentTime + 60000; // 60 seconds from now
    ctx.timerService().registerProcessingTimeTimer(timerTime);
}
```

### Deleting Timers
```java
// Delete event time timer
ctx.timerService().deleteEventTimeTimer(timerTimestamp);

// Delete processing time timer
ctx.timerService().deleteProcessingTimeTimer(timerTimestamp);
```

## Complete Example: Session Detection

Detect sessions with 10-minute timeout:

```java
public class SessionDetector
    extends KeyedProcessFunction<Long, Event, SessionResult> {

    private static final long SESSION_TIMEOUT = 10 * 60 * 1000; // 10 minutes

    // List of events in current session
    private transient ListState<Event> sessionEvents;

    // Timestamp of last event
    private transient ValueState<Long> lastEventTime;

    // Currently registered timer
    private transient ValueState<Long> timerState;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) throws Exception {
        ListStateDescriptor<Event> eventsDescriptor =
            new ListStateDescriptor<>("sessionEvents", Event.class);
        sessionEvents = getRuntimeContext().getListState(eventsDescriptor);

        ValueStateDescriptor<Long> timeDescriptor =
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("lastEventTime", Long.class);
        lastEventTime = getRuntimeContext().getState(timeDescriptor);

        ValueStateDescriptor<Long> timerDescriptor =
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("timer", Long.class);
        timerState = getRuntimeContext().getState(timerDescriptor);
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(
        Event event,
        Context ctx,
        Collector<SessionResult> out
    ) throws Exception {
        // Add event to session
        sessionEvents.add(event);

        // Update last event time
        lastEventTime.update(event.getTimestamp());

        // Delete old timer if exists
        Long oldTimer = timerState.value();
        if (oldTimer != null) {
            ctx.timerService().deleteEventTimeTimer(oldTimer);
        }

        // Register new timer for session timeout
        long newTimer = event.getTimestamp() + SESSION_TIMEOUT;
        ctx.timerService().registerEventTimeTimer(newTimer);
        timerState.update(newTimer);
    }

    @Override
    public void onTimer(
        long timestamp,
        OnTimerContext ctx,
        Collector<SessionResult> out
    ) throws Exception {
        // Session ended - collect all events and emit result
        List<Event> events = new ArrayList<>();
        for (Event e : sessionEvents.get()) {
            events.add(e);
        }

        if (!events.isEmpty()) {
            long sessionStart = events.get(0).getTimestamp();
            long sessionEnd = events.get(events.size() - 1).getTimestamp();

            out.collect(new SessionResult(
                ctx.getCurrentKey(),
                events.size(),
                sessionStart,
                sessionEnd
            ));
        }

        // Clear session state
        sessionEvents.clear();
        lastEventTime.clear();
        timerState.clear();
    }
}
```

## Example: Counting Events with Timeout

Count events and emit result after 1 minute of inactivity:

```java
public class TimeoutCounter
    extends KeyedProcessFunction<String, Event, Tuple2<String, Integer>> {

    private static final long TIMEOUT = 60000; // 1 minute

    private transient ValueState<Integer> countState;
    private transient ValueState<Long> timerState;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) throws Exception {
        countState = getRuntimeContext().getState(
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("count", Integer.class, 0)
        );
        timerState = getRuntimeContext().getState(
            new ValueStateDescriptor<>("timer", Long.class)
        );
    }

    @Override
    public void processElement(
        Event event,
        Context ctx,
        Collector<Tuple2<String, Integer>> out
    ) throws Exception {
        // Increment count
        Integer count = countState.value();
        count++;
        countState.update(count);

        // Delete old timer
        Long oldTimer = timerState.value();
        if (oldTimer != null) {
            ctx.timerService().deleteEventTimeTimer(oldTimer);
        }

        // Register new timer
        long newTimer = event.getTimestamp() + TIMEOUT;
        ctx.timerService().registerEventTimeTimer(newTimer);
        timerState.update(newTimer);
    }

    @Override
    public void onTimer(
        long timestamp,
        OnTimerContext ctx,
        Collector<Tuple2<String, Integer>> out
    ) throws Exception {
        // Timeout - emit count
        Integer count = countState.value();
        out.collect(new Tuple2<>(ctx.getCurrentKey().toString(), count));

        // Clear state
        countState.clear();
        timerState.clear();
    }
}
```

## Example: Deduplicate Within Time Window

Remove duplicate events within a 5-minute window:

```java
public class Deduplicator
    extends KeyedProcessFunction<String, Event, Event> {

    private static final long WINDOW = 5 * 60 * 1000; // 5 minutes

    // Store seen event IDs with their expiration time
    private transient MapState<String, Long> seenIds;

    @Override
    public void open(Configuration parameters) throws Exception {
